      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">What a Teacher Adds When You're Playing for Yourself</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Plenty of people learn a lot on their own, and self-directed exploration is a good thing. What a teacher adds is perspective you can't easily get from the inside:</p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 pl-5 list-none">
        {[
          "Noticing things you can't diagnose yet, like a tight thumb that's affecting your tone",
          "Sequencing skills so you aren't trying to solve everything at once",
          "Explaining a technique a different way when the first explanation doesn't click",
          "Choosing music you're excited to play that also builds the technique you need",
          "Giving specific feedback, so the time you spend practicing has a clear direction",
        ].map(item => (
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-base text-ink/70 font-light leading-relaxed">
            <span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-purplePrimary opacity-60 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            {item}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">When you're playing for yourself, that guidance doesn't need to point toward a competition or an audition. It points toward your goals: the piece you want to play, the sound you want to make, the way you want playing to feel. In my studio, that means lessons built around the individual student, with real say over pacing and repertoire, and high standards for technique that serve your music rather than someone else's checklist.</p>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">So, When Are Violin Lessons Worth It?</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Violin lessons are likely worth it for you if you want to improve, you're curious about the instrument, you value knowledgeable feedback, and you're willing to spend some attentive, reasonably consistent time practicing between lessons.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">You don't need professional ambitions. You don't need to progress at anyone else's pace, whether that's a child prodigy or another adult you know. What you do need is enough engagement with the process for your teacher's feedback and your own practice to build on each other. That partnership is where lessons earn their value.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Playing for yourself is a legitimate reason to learn seriously. It may even be one of the best ones, because the music you're working toward is the music you actually care about.</p>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <h3 class="mt-8 font-heading font-semibold text-ink" style="font-size: 1.1rem; letter-spacing: -0.015em;">Are violin lessons worth it if I only want to play for fun?</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Yes, they can be. Lessons give you feedback, a clear sequence of skills, and help choosing what to work on next. They're most worthwhile when you also practice attentively and reasonably consistently between lessons, so the feedback has something to build on.</p>

      <h3 class="mt-8 font-heading font-semibold text-ink" style="font-size: 1.1rem; letter-spacing: -0.015em;">Do I need to practice every day for violin lessons to be worth it?</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">No. Missing days is normal. What matters is returning to the instrument consistently enough for learning to accumulate, and practicing with focus when you do. Short, attentive sessions are often more productive than long, distracted ones.</p>

      <h3 class="mt-8 font-heading font-semibold text-ink" style="font-size: 1.1rem; letter-spacing: -0.015em;">Can adults make meaningful progress on violin?</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Yes. Adults make meaningful progress when they combine good instruction with consistent, attentive practice. The pace varies from person to person, and comparing yourself to children or other adults usually tells you very little about your own progress.</p>

      <h3 class="mt-8 font-heading font-semibold text-ink" style="font-size: 1.1rem; letter-spacing: -0.015em;">What if some violin techniques take me much longer than others?</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">That's very common. Violin is made up of many skills, like bow control, intonation, rhythm, and reading, and each one develops at its own rate. A slower skill isn't a sign you lack ability. It usually just needs more focused practice and sometimes a different explanation.</p>

      <!-- Local SEO closing section -->
      <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-lavender-200">
